# UDAP metadata

The nx-fhir `auth-udap` feature makes this server publish signed UDAP metadata at `{baseURL}/.well-known/udap`. The metadata gives a client the endpoints of the authorization server of `app.auth.issuer`. That server registers clients, authorizes them, and issues tokens.

The metadata follows the HL7 FAST security IG (Security for Scalable Registration, Authentication, and Authorization), STU2 2.0.0. The feature needs the `auth-resource` feature.

## What the feature adds

The feature adds `UdapConfig`, `UdapProperties`, `UdapCertificate`, `UdapCertificateFetcher`, and `UdapMetadata` under `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/udap/`, and the unit tests `UdapMetadataTest`, `UdapCertificateFetcherTest`, and `UdapConfigTest` and the integration test `AuthUdapIT`, which runs under `mvn verify`, under `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/udap/`.

The install writes `app.auth.udap.enabled: false` to `src/test/resources/config/application.yaml` and to `src/test/resources/application-test.yaml`, so a test runs with the feature only when it sets `app.auth.enabled` and `app.auth.udap.enabled` to `true`.

## Configuration

The install writes the configuration below, the `udap` map of `app.auth`. `AUTH-RESOURCE.md` gives the other keys of `app.auth`, which this feature also uses: `enabled`, `issuer`, and `ssl-verify`.

```yaml
app:
  auth:
    udap:
      enabled: true
      fetch-cert: true
      profiles-supported:
        - udap_dcr
        - udap_authn
        - udap_authz
        - udap_to
      grant-types-supported:
        - authorization_code
        - refresh_token
        - client_credentials
      authorization-extensions-supported:
        - hl7-b2b
      authorization-extensions-required: []
      certifications-supported: []
      token-endpoint-auth-signing-alg-values-supported:
        - RS256
        - RS384
        - ES256
        - ES384
      registration-endpoint-jwt-signing-alg-values-supported:
        - RS256
        - RS384
        - ES256
        - ES384
```

With the `certFile` option, the map has `cert-file` and no `fetch-cert`. The keys:

- `enabled`: when it and `app.auth.enabled` are both `true`, the server gets its certificate at startup and publishes the metadata. When one of the two is `false` or absent, the feature registers no interceptor, loads no certificate, and makes no call, and HAPI FHIR answers a request to `/fhir/.well-known/udap` with status 404.
- `authorization-endpoint`, `token-endpoint`, and `registration-endpoint`: optional. A set value replaces the value from the UDAP metadata of the issuer.
- `scopes-supported`: an optional list. A list with an entry replaces the scope list from the UDAP metadata of the issuer.
- The UDAP lists `profiles-supported`, `grant-types-supported`, `authorization-extensions-supported`, `authorization-extensions-required`, `certifications-supported`, `certifications-required` (optional), `token-endpoint-auth-signing-alg-values-supported`, and `registration-endpoint-jwt-signing-alg-values-supported`: a list in the configuration replaces the list from the UDAP metadata of the issuer, also an empty list. For a list that is not in the configuration, the server uses the member with the same element name in the UDAP metadata of the issuer, for example `udap_profiles_supported`, only when that document has `udap_versions_supported`. An empty `-supported` list tells a client that the authorization server does not support that capability, and an empty `-required` list tells a client that no entry is required. The generated values are starter values. Write the values of your authorization server, or remove a list that the UDAP metadata of that server gives.
- `cert-file`: the path of a PKCS#12 file.
- `cert-password`: the password of the file. In the fetch route, it is the password that the server sends with the fetch, and `udap-test` when it is absent. A hosted container sets it with the environment variable `APP_AUTH_UDAP_CERTPASSWORD`.
- `fetch-cert`: `true` or `false`, and absent means `false`. With `true` and no `cert-file`, the server gets a certificate from the issuer.
- `fetch-cert-retry-attempts`: the number of fetch tries. The default is 10.
- `fetch-cert-retry-delay`: the time between two fetch tries, in milliseconds. The default is 5000.

`app.auth.ssl-verify` of the `auth-resource` feature also applies to the certificate fetch and to the read of the UDAP metadata of the issuer. With `false`, the server accepts any certificate and any host name for these calls.

WARNING: With `app.auth.ssl-verify: false`, the server accepts any certificate from the hosts it calls. Use it only with a local development certificate.

### IG rules for the lists

When you write a list, obey these rules of the IG:

- `profiles-supported` has `udap_dcr` and `udap_authn`.
- When `grant-types-supported` has `client_credentials`, `profiles-supported` has `udap_authz`.
- Each entry of `authorization-extensions-required` is also in `authorization-extensions-supported`.
- Each entry of `certifications-required` is also in `certifications-supported`.
- `grant-types-supported` has `refresh_token` only when it also has `authorization_code`.
- When `profiles-supported` has `udap_to`, the published scope list has `udap`.

## Base URL

The class `FhirBaseUrl` of the `auth-resource` feature gives the base URL. It is the value of `hapi.fhir.server_address` with one trailing `/` removed. With no value there, it is `http://localhost:`, then the value of `server.port` (`8080` when absent), then the servlet context path with one trailing `/` removed, then `/fhir`. The base URL is the `iss` and the `sub` of the signed metadata, and the certificate fetch sends it as the name of the certificate.

## Certificate

At startup, with `app.auth.enabled` and `app.auth.udap.enabled` both `true`, the server gets its certificate by one of two routes:

1. With `cert-file` set, the server loads that file with `cert-password`.
2. With no `cert-file` and with `fetch-cert: true`, the server loads `generated-cert.pfx` from its working directory. When that file is absent, the server sends `POST {issuer}/api/cert/generate` with the base URL and the password. Each try has a connect timeout of 5 seconds and a limit of 30 seconds for the complete response. After a try succeeds, the server writes the response to `generated-cert.pfx` one time. An error of that write stops startup with no new try.

The server fetches a certificate only at startup.

The server uses the first key entry of the file. The key must be an RSA key of 2048 bits or more. The `x5c` header of the signed metadata has the certificate chain of that entry, leaf first. When the leaf certificate is not valid or has no URI alternative name equal to the base URL, startup continues with one WARN line, and a UDAP client rejects the signed metadata.

The file `generated-cert.pfx` holds a private key. The generator adds the file name to the `.gitignore` of the server project. When you change the base URL or `app.auth.issuer`, delete `generated-cert.pfx`, and the next start fetches a new certificate.

WARNING: A fetched certificate comes from a test authority, and its private key is made by a different server. Use the fetch only for development.

## Where each metadata value comes from

Each element takes the value of the property with the same name, for example `profiles-supported` for `udap_profiles_supported`, or else the value from the UDAP metadata of the issuer. These elements differ:

- `udap_versions_supported` is `["1"]`, and `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported` is `["private_key_jwt"]`.
- `udap_authorization_extensions_required` and `udap_certifications_required` are `[]` when neither source has them. Each is present when its `-supported` element has an entry.
- `scopes_supported` comes from the UDAP metadata of the issuer when `scopes-supported` is empty.
- `authorization_endpoint` is present only when `grant_types_supported` has `authorization_code`.
- `signed_metadata` is the signed metadata.

The class `IssuerMetadata` of the `auth-resource` feature reads `{issuer}/.well-known/udap` at the first metadata request that needs it and keeps the document after a successful read. The server reads that document only when a value that it publishes has no property. The read has a connect timeout of 5 seconds and a read timeout of 10 seconds.

The signed metadata is a JWT signed with RS256 and the key of the certificate, with the certificate chain in the `x5c` header. Its `iss` and `sub` are the base URL, `iat` is the time of the request, `exp` is 24 hours after `iat`, and `jti` is a new random UUID for each response. `token_endpoint` and `registration_endpoint` have the values of the plain metadata, and `authorization_endpoint` is there when the plain metadata has it.

## Request

The class `WellKnownInterceptor` of the `auth-resource` feature answers the request.

- `GET {baseURL}/.well-known/udap` gets the metadata as one JSON object with status 200 and `Content-Type: application/json`.
- The request needs no token. The `auth-resource` feature lets each request under `/fhir/.well-known/` pass.
- Each `community` value gets the same metadata.
- Only `GET` gets the metadata. A request with a different method goes on to HAPI FHIR.
- When a value that the metadata must have comes from no property and not from the UDAP metadata of the issuer, the response has status 503. Each list that is not a `-required` list is such a value. The log has the reason.
- When the read of the UDAP metadata of the issuer fails, the response has status 503. The log has the reason. The next request reads it again.

## What stops startup

With `app.auth.enabled` and `app.auth.udap.enabled` both `true`, the application stops at startup in each of these cases. The message names the key, the file, or the URL of the certificate fetch.

- `cert-file` is absent, and `fetch-cert` is `false` or absent.
- `cert-file` is set and `cert-password` is not. The message also names the environment variable `APP_AUTH_UDAP_CERTPASSWORD`.
- The file of `cert-file` does not load, or its key is not an RSA key.
- The server makes a fetch, and `app.auth.issuer` is not an absolute `http` or `https` URL.
- The fetch fails after the last try, or the write of the fetched certificate fails.
- The saved `generated-cert.pfx` does not load, or its key is not an RSA key. Delete the file. Then the next start fetches a certificate.
- The key of the certificate cannot sign the metadata. The signer refuses an RSA key shorter than 2048 bits.
